=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch_server_access
diagnosis_text:
The entire FRR routing stack on switch_server_access has crashed or been
killed: systemctl reports frr.service as "inactive (dead)". Because FRR is
down, ospfd is not running on this device even though its on-disk
configuration (/etc/frr/frr.conf) is correctly written — it declares a
router-id of 172.16.0.11, places 10.200.0.0/24 into OSPF Area 0, and
places the uplink 172.16.0.10/31 into Area 0 as well.

With ospfd dead, switch_server_access cannot form an OSPF adjacency with
router_core_3 over their shared eth2/eth5 link. Stale LSAs from
switch_server_access (router-LSA and network-LSA, both ~378 s old) are
still sitting in the Area 0 LSDB, which is why router_core_1, router_core_2,
and router_core_3 still transiently show an OSPF route to 10.200.0.0/24 in
their RIBs. However, those stale LSAs are no longer reachable in the SPF
topology — router_core_3 has already re-originated its router-LSA without
the link to switch_server_access — so router_core_1 (the ABR between Area 0
and Area 0.0.0.1) does not generate a type-3 summary LSA for 10.200.0.0/24
into Area 0.0.0.1. As a result, switch_dist_1_1 and switch_dist_2_1 have no
route to the server subnet at all, and every packet from the hosts destined
for 10.200.0.x is dropped with ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable" at the
distribution layer (sourced from 10.1.1.1 / switch_dist_1_1).

Root cause: frr.service is inactive/dead on switch_server_access, disabling
OSPF and removing 10.200.0.0/24 from the reachable topology for the two user
pods.

Corrective fix: Log in to switch_server_access and run
`systemctl start frr` (or `systemctl restart frr`). This will bring ospfd
back up using the existing correct frr.conf, re-establish the adjacency with
router_core_3, re-advertise 10.200.0.0/24 into Area 0, and allow
router_core_1 to regenerate the type-3 summary into Area 0.0.0.1, restoring
full reachability to the data-center server segment within OSPF convergence
time.
=== END ===
